@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+# AI and integration scope audit
+
+This is a code audit and an implementation boundary for the employee-rights specification, version 2. It is not a report of production configuration or a release approval. No real rights, credentials, connections, customer messages, or imports were changed during this work.
+
+## Implemented without expanding authority
+
+1. AI agents now have a separate, hidden `access_responsible_id`. Historical rows use the same one-time unambiguous company-owner migration as native documents. `created_by` remains historical, and new user-created agents store the authenticated creator as the responsible member. Browser patches cannot change that field.
+2. AI schema readiness checks include the ownership column, tenant-composite FK, index, and durable migration receipt. A cold schema repair does not reinterpret new NULL-owned system rows as historical documents.
+3. The AI configuration-options GET checks current database management authority before its optional Kommo catalog request. A logged-in session without AI-management authority does not trigger a provider request. Remote failure can still use the already authorized local catalog; a successful refresh reauthorizes in its store transaction.
+4. Tenant-wide handoff diagnostics, Connect diagnostics, and the unified AI-state snapshot are blocked for a member with an explicit narrow/denied dialog read scope until those aggregate projections are partitioned by source. Their existing legacy and tenant-wide paths are unchanged. This closes an indirect metadata bypass; it does not grant AI management through dialog rights.
+5. No AI or integrations row is published in `SCOPE_ACTION_CATALOG`. The global employee-scopes rollout gate remains off by default.
+
+## Integration management: why generic CRUD is not ready
+
+| Surface | Current authority and storage | Unresolved scope boundary |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Kommo OAuth/manual connection and disconnect | Owner-only route/store checks; one credential row per tenant/provider, encrypted secret; callback uses a one-use state. | The connection serves multiple pipelines/employees. A responsible employee for a shared credential is not defined. An employee-level delete/update could disconnect or replace everybody's connection. OAuth consent and token lifetime remain separate from data access. |
+| BeinSystems ERP connection / primary provider | Owner management; tenant/provider credential row and shared primary-provider selection. | A personal/department scope cannot safely control shared credentials or change the company's primary CRM without a defined domain rule. Do not connect the ERP as part of this task. |
+| Linear settings and binding | Owner-only management; team/account/member bindings govern distinct task/news operations. | Integration configuration is not the same as a member's Linear-task permissions or published-news read access. Existing binding constraints must not be converted to generic CRUD grants. |
+| Connect account setup, webhooks, disconnect, channel retirement/reconciliation | Existing owner/admin and stronger operation-specific protections; account-level API key and webhook may cover several channels. | Dialog source-to-employee mappings authorize dialog data, not replacement of the shared key/webhook. Account disconnect, one-channel disconnect, retirement, and identity reconciliation have different effects and cannot all be an inferred generic delete. |
+| Chat AI provider configuration | Existing owner/admin checks and stricter owner-only fields; encrypted provider credential and company-wide model/consent/spend settings. | Employee access to an AI agent does not define authority over company credentials, provider consent, limits, or every agent's runtime. |
+
+Read-only integration status is already a separate behavior: the base tenant list and Connect status can expose safe connection metadata to an active member, whereas some provider-specific management pages require the owner. Silently mapping all such reads to one owner-based matrix could remove existing access. The migration must compare these current behaviors rather than assume they are identical.
+
+Primary sources: `tenant_integration_store.py`, `tenant_kommo_api.py`, `tenant_bein_integration.py`, `tenant_bein_api.py`, `tenant_linear_policy.py`, `tenant_linear_api.py`, `tenant_connect_transport.py`, `tenant_connect_api.py`, `tenant_connect_retirement.py`, `tenant_connect_direct_reconcile.py`, `tenant_chat_ai_settings.py`, and route registration in `bot.py`.
+
+## AI agents: real operations and their indirect paths
+
+The core API has real create/list/update/delete operations. Updating `enabled` is an existing agent update, not a new independent grant. However, row-filtering only `list_agents` and `save_agent` would leave the following connected paths unprotected:
+
+- Agent triggers, teams, primary/expert bindings, and replacement/archive operations reference more than one agent.
+- Knowledge sources can be company-wide or agent-bound. Upload, archive, restore, deduplication, and publication have existing separate constraints.
+- Dashboard, reliability, handoffs, channel catalog, state snapshots, and deletion previews aggregate agents, dialogs, jobs, identities, or usage beyond a single agent row.
+- Virtual tests, simulations, historical replay, instructor/learning workflows, analysis campaigns, and AI-administrator drafts/approvals can read configuration and execute operations indirectly.
+- Pause/resume/handoff and dialog assignments require source access and independent AI authority. Broad AI-management privilege must not override a newly explicit narrow dialog scope. The dialog-scope implementation handles exact-source conversation endpoints separately from the aggregate guards above.
+- Company emergency stop, automatic sending, native CRM actions, extraction and provider spending are shared runtime controls. Permission over an employee-owned agent does not automatically authorize changing them for the company.
+- Permanent deletion currently requires company-owner authority, exact-agent confirmation, expected version, and safety checks against active work. It must not become an ordinary employee operation merely because a generic D cell exists.
+- Background workers execute under admitted jobs/runtime safety policy, not a current interactive employee request. Revocation, queued work, creator identity, and post-revocation execution must be resolved explicitly before using employee scopes as a worker policy.
+
+The common AI store `_authorize` currently means company owner or active admin with the existing `ai_agents_manage` flag. Many independent modules use it. Replacing it with one Boolean “some scoped action is allowed” would make view/create/update interchangeable and would bypass both resource scope and operation-specific authority.
+
+Primary sources: `tenant_ai_agents_store.py`, `tenant_ai_agents_api.py`, `tenant_ai_dashboard.py`, `tenant_ai_state.py`, `tenant_ai_reliability.py`, `tenant_ai_testing_store.py`, `tenant_ai_simulation_engine.py`, `tenant_ai_historical_replay.py`, `tenant_ai_instructor_store.py`, `tenant_ai_learning.py`, `tenant_ai_administrator_apply.py`, `tenant_knowledge_candidates_store.py`, `tenant_operator_assignment.py`, and `tenant_web_routes.py`.
+
+## Required decisions and remaining implementation
+
+- Define integration-management ownership for shared credentials and distinguish per-source data access from account-wide connection effects.
+- Specify the treatment of shared AI/knowledge/runtime resources and cross-agent operations, including the retained owner-only safety actions.
+- Decide which related-dialog/AI aggregate information should remain available inside an authorized AI document versus its general section. Narrow-dialog users currently receive a deliberate blocked response for unconverted tenant-wide diagnostics.
+- Enumerate every interactive and background route for each registered action, apply the same effective scope, and compare rights before/after on a synthetic copy before activating the matrix.
+
+The current work supplies safe ownership metadata and closes concrete authorization gaps. It does **not** claim that AI/integration CRUD scopes are fully implemented.
